/* Déclic — Anglais B2 (unités 1 à 3). Format : docs/ANGLAIS.md */
(()=>{
// Toutes les variantes d'une réponse : V('Do you know {w} it is?', {w:['if','whether']})
const V=(t,o={})=>Object.entries(o).reduce((a,[k,vs])=>a.flatMap(s=>vs.map(v=>s.split('{'+k+'}').join(v))),[t]);
const paras=t=>t.split('\n\n').map(x=>`<p>${x}</p>`).join('');

const READ_IMPR=`Seven seconds. That is how long, according to a piece of advice repeated in countless careers guides, you have to make a good first impression. The real figure may be even shorter. In one often-quoted experiment, volunteers were shown photographs of strangers’ faces for just a tenth of a second and asked how trustworthy, competent or likeable each person seemed. Their snap judgements turned out to be remarkably similar to those of people who had been allowed to study the same faces for as long as they wanted.

Speed, however, is not the same as accuracy. We are quick to decide what someone is like, but our verdicts are shaped by things that have little to do with character: a firm handshake, a confident voice, a resemblance to someone we already know. Confidence is a particular trap. People who come across as self-assured are often assumed to be competent, even when there is no evidence for it, while quieter people tend to be underestimated.

What makes first impressions so powerful is not that they are formed quickly but that they are hard to undo. Once we have decided that a new colleague is arrogant, we notice everything that confirms it and overlook everything that doesn’t. Psychologists call this confirmation bias, and it explains why a bad start can follow someone for months.

So what can we do? Recruiters who have learnt the lesson tend to ask every candidate the same questions in the same order, and to write down their impressions only at the end. In everyday life, the advice is simpler: notice your first impression, then ask yourself what it is actually based on. You may find that the answer is: not very much.`;

const READ_PAST=`My grandparents ran a corner shop in a small town in the north of England for almost forty years. When I was a child, I would spend every school holiday there. I’d sit on an upturned crate behind the counter, and my grandfather would let me weigh out sweets for the customers, a job I took extremely seriously.

He used to know everyone’s name, and most people’s secrets. Customers would come in for a pint of milk and stay for half an hour. Back then, I assumed this was simply what shops were like. It was only years later, when I moved to a city where nobody in my building knew my name, that I understood how unusual it had been.

The shop closed in the spring of 2009. Business had been getting worse for years: a supermarket had opened on the edge of town, and my grandparents, who were both over seventy by then, had been working six days a week without a proper holiday for longer than anyone could remember. My grandmother told me later that they had been thinking about retiring for a decade, but they had kept putting it off because they couldn’t imagine who would look after their customers.

On the last day, people queued outside the door. Some brought flowers; one man brought a cake he had baked himself. My grandfather, who was not a sentimental man, had to go into the back room for a few minutes. I had never seen him cry before, and I never saw him cry again.

Looking back, I don’t think I was nostalgic for the shop itself. I was nostalgic for a kind of town that no longer exists.`;

const READ_FUT=`“Where do you see yourself in five years’ time?” It is one of the most predictable interview questions, and one of the most dreaded. Answer too modestly and you seem unambitious; answer too boldly (“In your job”) and you risk sounding arrogant, or simply odd.

Recruiters who ask it are not, for the most part, expecting a precise prediction. Nobody knows what they will be doing in five years, and a good interviewer knows that. What they want to find out is whether your plans and theirs are heading in the same direction. If the role is a stepping stone to something you clearly care about, that is usually fine. If you are obviously going to hand in your notice the moment something better comes along, it is not.

The most convincing answers tend to describe skills rather than titles. “By then, I’d like to have led a project from start to finish” says more than “I’ll be a senior manager”, because it shows what you will have learnt, not just where you will be sitting. It also sounds more realistic. Promotions depend on many things you cannot control; experience depends largely on you.

It is also worth thinking about the question for your own sake, not just for the interview. Imagine it is five years from now. What will you be doing on an ordinary Tuesday morning? What will you have achieved that you have not achieved yet? Which of your current worries will you have forgotten about completely? If the answers are vague, that is not a failure. It may simply mean that you are keeping your options open, which, in a world that changes as quickly as ours, may be the most sensible plan of all.`;

COURS.push(
/* ======================================================================= */
{subj:'en', sem:'b2', id:'en-b2-impressions', o:1, h:'First impressions', p:'Ask delicate questions tactfully, end a question with a preposition as native speakers do, and describe people with precision, nuance and a little diplomacy.', steps:[
 {kind:'Écoute', h:'After the interviews',
  p:`<p>By the end of this unit, you will be able to ask sensitive questions politely, describe people with nuance rather than in black and white, and talk about the impression someone made on you, and how it changed.</p>
<p>Hannah and Raj have just interviewed two candidates for a team leader position. <b>Listen without reading</b>, twice if you need to. The transcript will appear once you have answered.</p>
<p><b>Question:</b> which candidate are they leaning towards, and why?</p>`,
  audio:[['Hannah','So, what did you make of the last one? Daniel, was it?'],['Raj','Daniel Okafor. Honestly, I’m in two minds. He came across as very self-assured, maybe a little too polished.'],['Hannah','I know what you mean. Every answer sounded rehearsed. When I asked him how he’d handle a project that was going off the rails, he practically recited a management textbook.'],['Raj','Whereas Sophie was the opposite. She was rather reserved at first. For the first ten minutes, I wasn’t sure she even wanted the job.'],['Hannah','She warmed up, though. And did you notice what she asked us at the end? She wanted to know how the team deals with disagreements. Nobody ever asks that.'],['Raj','True. She strikes me as someone who listens before she speaks. Do you remember who she was working for before?'],['Hannah','A small design agency in Leeds. She was managing a team of eight there.'],['Raj','Then I think she’s our best bet. She’s not exactly charismatic, but I’d trust her judgement.'],['Hannah','Agreed. Could you find out whether she’s free for a second interview next Thursday?']], cache:true,
  opts:['Sophie, because she seemed thoughtful and asked a perceptive question.','Daniel, because he was confident and very well prepared.','Sophie, because she was the most charismatic candidate.','Neither of them: they want to advertise the job again.'], ans:0,
  why:'Raj says Sophie <i>strikes me as someone who listens before she speaks</i>, and Hannah was impressed by her question about disagreements. Daniel came across as <i>too polished</i>: his answers sounded rehearsed. Notice that Raj calls Sophie <i>not exactly charismatic</i>: a polite way of saying she isn’t charismatic at all.'},

 {kind:'Vocabulaire', h:'Making an impression',
  p:`<p>These expressions come from the conversation or are closely related to it. Tap a line to hear it. Learn them as whole chunks: native speakers store and reuse them as ready-made blocks, and so should you. They will come back in your vocabulary review.</p>`,
  mots:[['to come across as','donner l’impression d’être, paraître','He comes across as arrogant, but he’s actually very shy.'],['to strike someone as','faire à quelqu’un l’effet de','She strikes me as someone you can rely on.'],['What did you make of…?','Qu’as-tu pensé de… ?','What did you make of the new manager?'],['to be in two minds (about)','hésiter, être partagé (sur)','I’m in two minds about accepting the offer.'],['to make a good first impression','faire bonne première impression','You only get one chance to make a good first impression.'],['to warm up (to someone)','se dégeler, se détendre (avec quelqu’un)','The children took a while to warm up to their new teacher.'],['to put someone at ease','mettre quelqu’un à l’aise','He has a gift for putting people at ease.'],['to judge a book by its cover','juger sur les apparences','Don’t judge a book by its cover: he’s much kinder than he looks.'],['to give someone the benefit of the doubt','accorder à quelqu’un le bénéfice du doute','She was late again, but let’s give her the benefit of the doubt.'],['polished','soigné, rodé (parfois trop lisse)','His presentation was polished but a bit soulless.'],['rehearsed','récité, appris par cœur','Her answers sounded rehearsed.'],['your best bet','la meilleure option','If you want a quiet table, your best bet is to book early.']],
  note:'<i>What did you make of him?</i> asks for your overall judgement, often after some hesitation; <i>What did you think of him?</i> is a more neutral equivalent. Both are followed by an opinion, not a description: <i>I wasn’t very impressed</i>, <i>I thought he was great</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a first impression',
  p:'Translate into English, using <i>come across as</i>: « Il donne l’impression d’être très sûr de lui. » <b>Question:</b> how would you say it?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:V('He comes across as {b}very {a}.',{b:['','being '],a:['self-assured','confident','self-confident','sure of himself']}),
  why:'<i>He comes across as very self-assured.</i> <i>come across as</i> is followed directly by an adjective (or a noun phrase: <i>he comes across as a nice guy</i>). It describes the impression, not necessarily the truth.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Indirect questions: asking without being blunt',
  p:`<p>Hannah did not ask <i>“Is she free next Thursday?”</i> She said: <i>Could you find out <b>whether she’s free</b> next Thursday?</i> And Raj did not ask <i>“Who was she working for?”</i> but <i>Do you remember <b>who she was working for</b>?</i> These are <b>indirect questions</b>: a question placed inside a longer sentence. They sound more polite and more tentative, less like an interrogation, which is why English speakers use them constantly with strangers, at work, and whenever the topic is delicate.</p>
<div class="def"><p>An indirect question = an <b>opening phrase</b> + a <b>question word</b> (or <b>if / whether</b>) + <b>subject + verb in statement order</b>.</p><ul><li>Openings: <i>Could you tell me…? Do you know…? Do you happen to know…? Would you mind telling me…? I was wondering… I’d like to know… I’m not sure…</i></li><li>Wh-questions keep their question word: <i>Where is the lift?</i> → <i>Do you know where <b>the lift is</b>?</i></li><li>Yes/no questions use <b>if</b> or <b>whether</b>: <i>Is it open?</i> → <i>Do you know <b>if / whether it’s</b> open?</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>Inside the indirect question there is no more inversion and no more <i>do / does / did</i>: the tense moves onto the main verb, exactly as in a statement. <i>What time does it start?</i> → <i>Could you tell me what time it <b>starts</b>?</i> <i>Why did she resign?</i> → <i>I wonder why she <b>resigned</b>.</i></p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>How much does the course cost?</i> → <i>Would you mind telling me how much the course costs?</i><br><i>Has the parcel arrived?</i> → <i>I was wondering whether the parcel had arrived.</i> (After a past opening, the tense often shifts back, as in reported speech.)</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> en français, on dit « Pouvez-vous me dire où <b>est</b> la gare ? », avec le verbe avant le sujet. En anglais, l’ordre redevient celui d’une affirmation : <i>Can you tell me where the station <b>is</b>?</i> et non <s>where is the station</s>. De même, pas de <i>do/does/did</i> : <i>Do you know what he <b>does</b>?</i> et non <s>what does he do</s>. Le point d’interrogation final dépend de l’ouverture : <i>Do you know where it is?</i> mais <i>I wonder where it is.</i></p></div>`,
  audio:'Could you tell me where the station is? Do you know if the museum is open on Mondays? I was wondering whether you had a moment.'},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'Turning a direct question into an indirect one',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> whenever you speak to someone you don’t know well, ask for a favour, or raise a sensitive subject. <b>Why it works:</b> the opening phrase softens the request, and the statement order signals that this is no longer a blunt question but a request for information. <b>How:</b></p>
<ol><li>Choose an opening that suits the situation: <i>Do you know…</i> is neutral; <i>I was wondering…</i> and <i>Would you mind telling me…</i> are more tentative.</li><li>Keep the question word. If there is none (a yes/no question), add <b>if</b> or <b>whether</b>.</li><li>Put the subject before the verb and remove <i>do / does / did</i>, transferring the tense to the main verb.</li><li>End with a question mark only if the opening itself is a question.</li></ol>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Make these questions more polite: a) <i>Where does the director live?</i> (start with <i>Do you know…</i>) b) <i>Did they sign the contract?</i> (start with <i>I was wondering…</i>)',
   etapes:[{f:'a) <i>Do you know</i> + <i>where</i> …',w:'The opening is a question, and the question word <i>where</i> stays.'},
    {f:'a) <i>Do you know where the director <b>lives</b>?</i>',w:'<i>does</i> disappears and its <i>-s</i> moves onto <i>live</i>. Subject (<i>the director</i>) before the verb.'},
    {f:'b) <i>I was wondering</i> + <i>whether</i> …',w:'<i>Did they sign…?</i> is a yes/no question, so we need <i>whether</i> (or <i>if</i>).'},
    {f:'b) <i>I was wondering whether they <b>had signed</b> the contract.</i>',w:'<i>did</i> disappears and the past moves onto the verb (<i>signed</i>, or <i>had signed</i> after the past opening). The opening is a statement, so the sentence ends with a full stop.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>Do you know where the director lives? I was wondering whether they had signed the contract.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: could you tell me…?',
  p:'You are at a conference and need to find the main hall. Rewrite the question politely, starting with <i>Could you tell me…</i> <b>Question:</b> <i>Where is the main hall?</i>', lab:'Your question',
  txt:V('Could you {p}tell me where the main hall is{q}?',{p:['','please '],q:['',', please']}),
  why:'<i>Could you tell me where the main hall <b>is</b>?</i> The verb comes after the subject. <s>Could you tell me where is the main hall?</s> is the classic French-speaker error.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: if or whether',
  p:'You want to check something with a hotel receptionist. Rewrite the question starting with <i>Do you know…</i> <b>Question:</b> <i>Does the price include breakfast?</i>', lab:'Your question',
  txt:[...V('Do you know {w} the price includes breakfast{o}?',{w:['if','whether'],o:['',' or not']}),'Do you know whether or not the price includes breakfast?'],
  why:'A yes/no question needs <i>if</i> or <i>whether</i>. <i>does</i> disappears and <i>include</i> takes the <i>-s</i>: <i>Do you know if the price includes breakfast?</i>'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Put the polite question in order',
  p:'Tap the words in the right order. <b>Question:</b> how do you ask a colleague, very tentatively, when the meeting finishes?',
  ordre:['Do you','happen','to know','what time','the meeting','finishes?'],
  why:'<i>Do you happen to know what time the meeting finishes?</i> <i>happen to</i> makes the question even more tentative (« par hasard »). Inside, statement order: <i>the meeting finishes</i>.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Who are you talking to? Prepositions at the end',
  p:`<p>Raj asked: <i>Do you remember who she was working <b>for</b>?</i> The preposition <i>for</i> sits alone at the end. That is completely normal in English, and in everyday speech it is the only natural option.</p>
<div class="def"><p>When a question asks about the object of a preposition (<i>talk <b>to</b> someone, wait <b>for</b> something, worry <b>about</b> something</i>), the question word goes at the front and the <b>preposition stays with its verb, at the end</b>:</p><ul><li><i>I’m waiting <b>for</b> my sister.</i> → <i>Who are you waiting <b>for</b>?</i></li><li><i>She’s from Cork.</i> → <i>Where’s she <b>from</b>?</i></li><li><i>It’s about a lighthouse keeper.</i> → <i>What’s the film <b>about</b>?</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>The verb and its preposition form a unit (<i>look at, depend on, apply for</i>), so English keeps them together. The formal alternative, <i>With whom did you travel?</i>, exists, but it belongs to official letters and legal texts. In conversation it sounds stiff, even comic. The same rule applies inside indirect questions: <i>Do you know who she’s working <b>for</b>?</i></p></div>
<div class="blk" data-t="Three questions not to confuse"><p><i>What’s she <b>like</b>?</i> = What kind of person is she? (personality, sometimes appearance) → <i>She’s quite shy, but very funny.</i><br><i>What does she <b>look like</b>?</i> = appearance only → <i>She’s tall, with short dark hair.</i><br><i>What does she like?</i> = her tastes → <i>She likes jazz and hiking.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> le français place la préposition en tête (« <b>Avec qui</b> pars-tu ? », « <b>De quoi</b> parle le livre ? »). Le calque <i>With who are you going?</i> est maladroit : dis <i>Who are you going <b>with</b>?</i> et <i>What’s the book <b>about</b>?</i> Et « Comment est-elle ? » se dit <i>What’s she like?</i>, jamais <s>How is she?</s>, qui veut dire « Comment va-t-elle ? ».</p></div>`,
  audio:'Who are you waiting for? Where is she from? What is the film about? What is she like?'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: ask about the missing part',
  p:'Your friend says: <i>I’m really worried about <u>the presentation</u>.</i> Ask the question whose answer is the underlined part, speaking to your friend directly. <b>Question:</b> what do you ask?', lab:'Your question',
  txt:['What are you worried about?','What are you so worried about?','What are you really worried about?'],
  why:'The preposition stays with <i>worried</i>, at the end: <i>What are you worried about?</i> <s>About what are you worried?</s> is not used.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Like, look like or be like?',
  p:'Your friend answers: <i>“Oh, he’s lovely. A bit shy at first, but really thoughtful, and very funny once you get to know him.”</i> <b>Question:</b> what did you ask?',
  opts:['What does he look like?','What does he like?','What’s he like?','How is he?'], ans:2,
  why:'The answer describes his personality, so the question was <i>What’s he like?</i> <i>What does he look like?</i> asks about appearance, <i>What does he like?</i> about his tastes, and <i>How is he?</i> about his health or mood.'},

 {kind:'Vocabulaire', h:'Describing people with nuance',
  p:`<p>At B2 level, describing someone is no longer a matter of <i>nice</i> or <i>not nice</i>. These adjectives and expressions let you be precise, and fair. Many are compound adjectives with a hyphen. Tap a line to hear it.</p>`,
  mots:[['self-assured','sûr de soi','She’s calm and self-assured in meetings.'],['level-headed','pondéré, qui garde la tête froide','We need someone level-headed in a crisis.'],['easy-going','facile à vivre, accommodant','My flatmate is very easy-going about housework.'],['outgoing','extraverti, sociable','He’s outgoing and makes friends easily.'],['down-to-earth','simple, les pieds sur terre','Despite her success, she’s very down-to-earth.'],['thoughtful','réfléchi ; attentionné','It was thoughtful of you to call.'],['short-tempered','soupe au lait, irascible','He can be short-tempered when he’s tired.'],['headstrong','têtu, qui n’en fait qu’à sa tête','She was a headstrong teenager.'],['likeable','sympathique','He’s a likeable guy, even if he talks too much.'],['sensible','raisonnable, sensé','That’s a very sensible decision.'],['sensitive','sensible, susceptible','He’s quite sensitive to criticism.'],['a bit on the quiet side','plutôt discret','She’s a bit on the quiet side, but she notices everything.']],
  note:'<b>Faux amis :</b> <i>sympathetic</i> veut dire « compatissant » (<i>She was very sympathetic when my father died</i>) ; « sympathique » se dit <i>nice, friendly, likeable</i>. <i>sensible</i> = raisonnable ; « sensible » se dit <i>sensitive</i>. <i>He has a strong character</i> se comprend, mais on dit plus naturellement <i>He has a strong personality</i> ou <i>He’s strong-willed</i>.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Saying it tactfully',
  p:`<p>Raj said Sophie was <i>not exactly charismatic</i>. He meant that she wasn’t charismatic at all, but saying so directly would have sounded harsh. English, and British English in particular, is full of these softening techniques. Recognising them is essential to understand what people really mean; using them makes you sound fluent and diplomatic.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>Softening adverbs</b> before a negative adjective: <i>a bit, a little, slightly, rather, somewhat</i>: <i>He can be <b>a bit</b> impatient.</i></li><li><b>Not + a positive adjective</b> instead of a negative one: <i>She’s <b>not very</b> organised</i> (= disorganised), <i>He’s <b>not exactly</b> punctual</i> (= he’s always late), <i>not the most patient person</i>.</li><li><b>Impression verbs</b> that make it your opinion rather than a fact: <i>come across as, strike someone as, seem, appear</i>: <i>He <b>comes across as</b> cold, but…</i></li><li><b>can be / tends to</b> to make it occasional: <i>She <b>tends to</b> interrupt.</i></li><li><b>-ish</b> for approximation: <i>He’s forty<b>ish</b>, tall<b>ish</b>, with grey<b>ish</b> hair.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="ex"><p>Blunt: <i>Mark is lazy and rude.</i><br>Tactful: <i>Mark isn’t the most hard-working person, and he can come across as a bit abrupt.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> <i>quite</i> est ambigu. En anglais britannique, <i>quite good</i> veut souvent dire « assez bien, sans plus », alors qu’en américain c’est plutôt « vraiment bien ». Et <i>a bit</i> se met devant un adjectif négatif (<i>a bit rude</i>) : devant un adjectif positif (<i>a bit brilliant</i>), il sonne bizarre ou ironique.</p></div>`,
  audio:'He can be a bit impatient. She’s not exactly punctual. He comes across as cold, but he’s actually very kind.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: soften a blunt remark',
  p:'A recruiter asks about a former colleague. You found him impolite, but you want to be diplomatic. Rewrite <i>He was rude.</i> using <b>not exactly</b> + an adjective meaning « poli ». <b>Question:</b> what do you say?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:V('He was not exactly {a}.',{a:['polite','courteous','well-mannered','diplomatic','tactful']}),
  why:'<i>He wasn’t exactly polite.</i> The meaning is clear (he was rude), but the tone is softer, and slightly ironic. Very British.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: false friends',
  p:'Translate into English: « Ma nouvelle collègue est très sympathique et très raisonnable. » <b>Question:</b> how do you say it?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:V('My new {c} is {i} {a} and {j} {b}.',{i:['very','really'],j:['very','really'],c:['colleague','co-worker','coworker'],a:['nice','friendly','likeable','pleasant'],b:['sensible','reasonable','level-headed']}),
  why:'<i>My new colleague is very nice and very sensible.</i> « Sympathique » = <i>nice, friendly, likeable</i> (pas <i>sympathetic</i>, compatissant) ; « raisonnable » = <i>sensible</i> (pas <i>sensitive</i>, sensible).'},

 {kind:'Prononciation', h:'Strong prepositions at the end',
  p:`<p>Prepositions such as <i>to, for, at, from, of</i> are usually unstressed. They take a <b>weak form</b> with the vowel /ə/, the “schwa”: <i>I’m talking <b>to</b> /tə/ Sam. It’s <b>for</b> /fə/ you. Look <b>at</b> /ət/ this.</i></p>
<div class="def"><p>But when the preposition ends a question or a sentence, it takes its <b>strong form</b>, with a full vowel: <i>Who are you talking <b>to</b> /tuː/? Who’s it <b>for</b> /fɔː/? What are you looking <b>at</b> /æt/? Where’s she <b>from</b> /frɒm/?</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> on prononce souvent toutes les prépositions en forme pleine, ce qui rend l’anglais haché. Réduis-les au milieu de la phrase (<i>a cup of tea</i> ≈ « a cup ə tea »), mais garde-les pleines en fin de question.</p></div>
<p>Listen to the contrast, then repeat the two questions. Make the final word clear and slightly longer.</p>`,
  audio:'I’m talking to Sam. Who are you talking to? It’s for you. Who’s it for? Look at this. What are you looking at?',
  dire:'Who are you talking to? What are you looking at?'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'Reading: the trouble with first impressions',
  p:`<p>Read the article, then answer. You can also listen to it.</p><div class="blk" data-t="Article">${paras(READ_IMPR)}</div><p><b>Question:</b> according to the article, what is the main problem with first impressions?</p>`,
  audio:READ_IMPR,
  opts:['They are formed too slowly to be useful.','Confident people always turn out to be incompetent.','They are hard to change, even when they are based on very little.','Recruiters ignore them completely.'], ans:2,
  why:'<i>What makes first impressions so powerful is not that they are formed quickly but that they are hard to undo</i>, and they are often based on superficial things (a handshake, a voice). The article says confident people are <i>assumed</i> to be competent, not that they never are.'},

 {kind:'Dictée', h:'Dictation: a polite question',
  p:'Listen and write exactly what you hear. You can listen as many times as you like and use “Plus lentement”.',
  dictee:'Do you happen to know whether the meeting has been moved to Thursday?',
  why:'Statement order after <i>whether</i>: <i>the meeting has been moved</i>. Check the spelling of <i>whether</i> (not <i>weather</i>, the rain and the sun).'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Repeat from memory',
  p:'The sentence is not written down. Listen to it, then say it from memory. Aim for the rhythm, not just the words: stressed syllables are longer and louder, and the small words are squeezed in between.',
  dire:{t:'She strikes me as someone who listens before she speaks.', cache:true}},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Talk about a first impression',
  p:'Speak for about one minute. Describe someone (a teacher, a colleague, a neighbour, a friend) whose first impression on you turned out to be wrong. Say what they seemed like at first, when and why your opinion changed, and what they are really like. Use at least one softening expression.',
  oral:{modele:'When I started my first job, my manager struck me as rather cold. She hardly ever smiled, she never made small talk, and to be honest, she came across as a bit arrogant. I was in two minds about whether I could work with her. Then, about a month later, I made a serious mistake with a client. I expected her to be furious, but she was completely level-headed. She asked me what had happened, helped me fix it, and never mentioned it again. That’s when I realised she wasn’t cold at all; she was just a bit on the quiet side. Now I’d say she’s one of the most thoughtful people I know. It taught me not to judge a book by its cover.',
   criteres:['I described the first impression with come across as, strike me as or seem','I explained when and why my opinion changed','I used at least one softening expression (a bit, not exactly, rather, on the quiet side…)','I used precise personality adjectives (level-headed, thoughtful, outgoing…)','I spoke for about a minute without long pauses']}},

 {kind:'Écriture', h:'Write an honest but tactful email',
  p:'Your friend Alex is about to join the team you used to work in, under a manager you know well. Write an email of about 180 words. Describe the manager honestly but tactfully (the first impression and the reality), give one or two pieces of advice, and ask Alex at least two indirect questions about the new job.',
  ecrire:{min:160, modele:'<p>Hi Alex,</p><p>Congratulations on the new job! I’m really pleased for you. You asked me what Helen is like, so here’s my honest opinion.</p><p>At first, she can come across as a bit distant. She doesn’t do small talk, and in your first meetings she might strike you as rather demanding. Don’t take it personally: that’s just how she is with everyone. Once you get to know her, you’ll find she’s fair, level-headed and surprisingly funny. She isn’t exactly patient with people who miss deadlines, though, so my advice is to tell her early if you’re running late with something. She hates surprises far more than problems. She also tends to say very little when she’s happy with your work, so silence is usually a good sign!</p><p>Anyway, I’d love to hear more about it. Do you know which projects you’ll be working on first? And I was wondering whether you’ll be based in the London office or working from home part of the week.</p><p>Good luck on Monday, and let me know how it goes!</p><p>Best,<br>Sam</p>',
   criteres:[{t:'I described a first impression (come across, strike, seem)', re:'\\b(come|comes|came) across\\b|\\bstr(ike|ikes|uck)\\b|\\bseem'},{t:'I softened at least one criticism', re:'\\b(a bit|a little|slightly|rather|somewhat|not exactly|exactly|not the most|tends? to|on the \\w+ side)\\b'},{t:'I asked at least one indirect question', re:'\\b(do you know|could you tell me|would you mind telling me|i was wondering|i wonder|do you happen to know|i[’\']d (love|like) to know)\\b'},'My indirect questions use statement order (where she is, not where is she)','I used precise personality adjectives','I wrote about 180 words, with a greeting and a closing']}},

 {kind:'Récapitulatif', h:'What to remember',
  p:`<div class="keep"><ul><li><b>Indirect questions</b>: opening (<i>Could you tell me / Do you know / I was wondering / Would you mind telling me</i>) + question word or <i>if / whether</i> + <b>subject + verb</b>, without <i>do / does / did</i>: <i>Do you know where she lives?</i> Question mark only if the opening is a question.</li><li><b>Prepositions at the end</b>: <i>Who are you waiting for? What’s it about? Where’s she from?</i> A fronted preposition (<i>With whom…</i>) is formal only. At the end of a question, the preposition takes its strong form.</li><li><i>What’s she like?</i> (personality) · <i>What does she look like?</i> (appearance) · <i>What does she like?</i> (tastes) · <i>How is she?</i> (health, mood).</li><li>Impressions: <i>come across as, strike someone as, make a good first impression, warm up to someone, What did you make of…?, be in two minds, give someone the benefit of the doubt</i>.</li><li>Nuance: <i>a bit / slightly / rather</i> + negative adjective; <i>not exactly / not very</i> + positive adjective; <i>can be, tends to, -ish</i>. Compound adjectives: <i>level-headed, self-assured, easy-going, down-to-earth, short-tempered</i>.</li><li>Faux amis : <i>sympathetic</i> = compatissant (sympathique = <i>nice, likeable</i>) ; <i>sensible</i> = raisonnable (sensible = <i>sensitive</i>).</li></ul></div>`},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'A phone call before your first day',
  p:'Lena is about to start a new job. Listen to her call with Mr Price from the HR department, then answer the questions one by one. You can listen again at any time.',
  audio:[['Mr Price','Good morning, Lena. Thanks for calling back. Is now a good time?'],['Lena','Yes, perfect. I just had a couple of questions about Monday, if that’s all right.'],['Mr Price','Of course. Go ahead.'],['Lena','Could you tell me what time I should arrive? The letter says nine, but I wasn’t sure whether that was for everyone.'],['Mr Price','Ah, good point. New starters come in at half past nine, so that you have time to collect your badge.'],['Lena','Great. And do you know who I’ll be reporting to in the first few weeks? I’ve heard the team leader is away.'],['Mr Price','That’s right. Until she gets back, you’ll be working with Owen Marsh. He’s very easy-going, and he’s been here for years, so he’s the person to ask about pretty much anything.'],['Lena','That’s reassuring. One last thing: I was wondering if there’s a dress code.'],['Mr Price','Not really. Smart casual. Nobody wears a tie unless they’re meeting clients.']], cache:true,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) What time should Lena arrive on Monday?', opts:['At nine o’clock','At half past eight','At half past nine','At ten o’clock'], ans:2, hint:'Mr Price corrects the time given in the letter.', why:'<i>New starters come in at half past nine</i>: 9.30, so that they have time to collect their badge.'},
   {q:'b) Which compound adjective does Mr Price use to describe Owen Marsh?', lab:'One word', txt:['easy-going','easygoing'], hint:'It means « facile à vivre ».', why:'<i>He’s very easy-going</i>: relaxed, not easily annoyed.'},
   {q:'c) Lena asked: <i>Could you tell me what time I should arrive?</i> Write the direct, less polite question she could have asked instead.', lab:'Direct question', txt:['What time should I arrive?','When should I arrive?','What time do I need to arrive?','What time do I have to arrive?'], hint:'Put the verb back before the subject: <i>should I…?</i>', why:'Direct question: <i>What time should I arrive?</i> (inversion). In the indirect version, statement order: <i>what time I should arrive</i>.'},
   {q:'d) You want to ask Mr Price, politely, where the staff canteen is. Start with <i>Do you know…</i>', lab:'Your question', txt:[...V('Do you know where the {c} is{p}?',{c:['staff canteen','canteen'],p:['',', please']})], hint:'Statement order: the subject, then the verb at the end.', why:'<i>Do you know where the staff canteen is?</i> and not <s>where is the staff canteen</s>.'}],
  sol:'<p>Lena should arrive at <b>half past nine</b>, not nine. Until the team leader comes back, she will work with Owen Marsh, who is <i>easy-going</i>. The dress code is smart casual. Her indirect questions (<i>Could you tell me what time I should arrive?</i>, <i>do you know who I’ll be reporting to?</i>, <i>I was wondering if there’s a dress code</i>) all use statement order; the direct version would be <i>What time should I arrive?</i> A polite question about the canteen: <i>Do you know where the staff canteen is?</i></p>'},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'A reference request',
  p:`<p>A former colleague of yours has applied for a job, and the recruiter has emailed you. Read the email and the notes you made about your colleague, then answer.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="Email"><p>Dear Ms Turner,</p><p>Tom Briggs has applied for the position of operations coordinator with us and has given your name as a referee. I was wondering whether you could spare ten minutes this week to tell us a little about him. In particular, we would like to know how long you worked together, what his main responsibilities were, and how he got on with the rest of the team. I would also be grateful if you could tell me whether you would work with him again.</p><p>Kind regards,<br>Priya Natarajan, Recruitment</p></div>
<div class="blk" data-t="Your notes on Tom"><p>Brilliant with figures and schedules. Never missed a deadline in three years. Not very patient with slower colleagues. Sometimes rude in meetings. Would work with him again, in the right role.</p></div>`,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) Which of these does the recruiter <b>not</b> ask about?', opts:['How long you worked with Tom','How Tom got on with his colleagues','Whether you would work with him again','How much Tom earned'], ans:3, hint:'Go through the email one question at a time.', why:'She asks how long you worked together, his responsibilities, how he got on with the team and whether you would work with him again. Nothing about his salary.'},
   {q:'b) The recruiter writes <i>how he got on with the rest of the team</i>. Write the direct question this indirect question comes from.', lab:'Direct question', txt:['How did he get on with the rest of the team?','How did he get along with the rest of the team?','How did Tom get on with the rest of the team?','How did Tom get along with the rest of the team?'], hint:'Past simple question: put <i>did</i> back and use the base form <i>get</i>.', why:'<i>How did he get on with the rest of the team?</i> In the indirect question, <i>did</i> disappears and <i>get</i> becomes <i>got</i>.'},
   {q:'c) Your notes say <i>Sometimes rude in meetings.</i> Rewrite it tactfully for the recruiter: start with <i>He</i>, use <b>can come across as</b>, and put a softening adverb before <i>abrupt</i>.', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('He can come across as {s} abrupt{e}.',{s:['a bit','a little','slightly','rather','somewhat'],e:['',' in meetings',' at times',' sometimes']}), hint:'He + can + come across as + (a bit / slightly…) + abrupt.', why:'<i>He can come across as a bit abrupt in meetings.</i> <i>can</i> makes it occasional, <i>come across as</i> makes it an impression, and <i>a bit</i> softens the adjective.'},
   {q:'d) Before replying, you want to know which team Tom would join. Ask the recruiter, using <i>would … be working</i> and putting the preposition <i>in</i> at the end.', lab:'Your question', txt:V('{w} team would {s} be working in?',{w:['Which','What'],s:['Tom','he']}), hint:'Question word + <i>team</i> at the front, the preposition <i>in</i> at the very end.', why:'<i>Which team would Tom be working in?</i> The formal version, <i>In which team would he be working?</i>, sounds stiff in an informal call.'}],
  sol:'<p>The recruiter wants to know how long you worked together, what Tom’s responsibilities were, how he got on with the team (<i>How did he get on with the rest of the team?</i>) and whether you would work with him again. Not his salary. A tactful version of your notes: <i>He’s brilliant with figures and schedules and never missed a deadline, but he can come across as a bit abrupt in meetings and isn’t always the most patient colleague. I would certainly work with him again, in the right role.</i> To find out more: <i>Which team would Tom be working in?</i></p>'},
]},

/* ======================================================================= */
{subj:'en', sem:'b2', id:'en-b2-past', o:2, h:'The past revisited', p:'Tell rich, layered stories about the past: the past perfect continuous, would and used to for old habits, be used to and get used to for adapting to change.', steps:[
 {kind:'Écoute', h:'A life in two places',
  p:`<p>By the end of this unit, you will be able to tell a story that moves back and forth in time, describe how life used to be, and talk about getting used to a new situation.</p>
<p>A podcast host is talking to Ruth, who left a career in London to live on a small Scottish island. <b>Listen without reading</b>, twice if you need to.</p>
<p><b>Question:</b> which statement best sums up Ruth’s experience?</p>`,
  audio:[['Host','Ruth, you’d been living in London for, what, twelve years when you decided to leave?'],['Ruth','Almost thirteen. I’d been working in advertising the whole time, and to be honest, I’d been feeling burnt out for ages before I admitted it to anyone, including myself.'],['Host','What was a typical day like back then?'],['Ruth','Oh, I’d get up at six, I’d be on the Tube by seven, and I’d rarely get home before nine. I used to think that was perfectly normal. Everyone I knew lived like that.'],['Host','And now you live on an island with, how many people?'],['Ruth','A hundred and eighty, in winter. It was a real shock at first. I wasn’t used to the silence. For the first few weeks I hardly slept: I kept waking up because there was no noise.'],['Host','But you got used to it.'],['Ruth','Eventually. Now I’m so used to it that London feels deafening whenever I go back. Funnily enough, I didn’t use to notice the noise at all.'],['Host','Any regrets?'],['Ruth','Only one. That I hadn’t done it ten years earlier.']], cache:true,
  opts:['She moved because she had lost her job in advertising.','She found the silence hard at first, but now London seems too loud to her.','She still can’t sleep properly on the island.','She regrets leaving London and plans to go back.'], ans:1,
  why:'<i>I wasn’t used to the silence… I hardly slept</i>, then <i>Now I’m so used to it that London feels deafening.</i> She left because she was <i>burnt out</i>, not because she lost her job, and her only regret is that she didn’t leave sooner.'},

 {kind:'Vocabulaire', h:'Change and memory',
  p:`<p>Expressions to talk about change, routine and the past. Tap a line to hear it. Notice how many are phrasal or fixed: <i>settle in, drift apart, come to terms with</i>. They will come back in your vocabulary review.</p>`,
  mots:[['to be burnt out','être épuisé, en burn-out','After two years without a holiday, she was completely burnt out.'],['the daily grind','le train-train, la routine usante','I don’t miss the daily grind of commuting.'],['back then','à l’époque','Back then, nobody had a mobile phone.'],['looking back','avec le recul','Looking back, I should have left sooner.'],['to make a fresh start','prendre un nouveau départ','After the divorce, he moved to Spain to make a fresh start.'],['to uproot yourself','tout quitter, se déraciner','It’s hard to uproot yourself after twenty years in one place.'],['to settle in','s’installer, prendre ses marques','How are you settling in?'],['to take some getting used to','demander un temps d’adaptation','Driving on the left takes some getting used to.'],['a turning point','un tournant','Losing that job was a turning point in my life.'],['it dawned on me that','j’ai pris conscience que','It dawned on me that I hadn’t had a day off in months.'],['to have second thoughts','avoir des doutes (après coup)','I had second thoughts the night before the move.'],['to come to terms with','accepter, se faire à','It took him years to come to terms with his father’s death.'],['out of the blue','sans crier gare, à l’improviste','She called me out of the blue after ten years.'],['to drift apart','s’éloigner peu à peu','We drifted apart after university.'],['a creature of habit','quelqu’un d’attaché à ses habitudes','My dad is a creature of habit: same café, same table, every day.'],['for ages','depuis une éternité, pendant des lustres','I’d been meaning to call you for ages.']],
  note:'<b>Burn-out :</b> le nom anglais s’écrit <i>burnout</i> ; l’adjectif est <i>burnt out</i> (britannique) ou <i>burned out</i> (américain). On ne dit pas <s>I’m in burn-out</s>, mais <i>I’m burnt out</i> ou <i>I’m suffering from burnout</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: it took some getting used to',
  p:'Translate into English, using <i>take some getting used to</i>: « Le silence a demandé un temps d’adaptation. » <b>Question:</b> how do you say it?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:V('The silence took {x} getting used to.',{x:['some','a bit of','a while','a lot of','quite a bit of']}),
  why:'<i>The silence took some getting used to.</i> The subject is the thing you have to adapt to, and <i>getting</i> is a gerund because <i>to</i> is a preposition here (more on that later in the unit).'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'The past perfect continuous',
  p:`<p>Ruth says: <i>I’d been working in advertising the whole time</i> and <i>I’d been feeling burnt out for ages before I admitted it.</i> She stands at a moment in the past (when she left London, when she admitted it) and looks further back at an activity that had been going on <b>up to that moment</b>. English has a tense for exactly this: the <b>past perfect continuous</b>.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>Form:</b> <i>had been</i> + verb-<i>ing</i>, the same for every person. Contracted: <i>I’d been, she’d been</i>. Negative: <i>hadn’t been working</i>. Question: <i>How long had you been waiting?</i></p></div>
<div class="idea"><p>It is the past version of the present perfect continuous. Compare: <i>I’ve been waiting for an hour</i> (up to now) → <i>I’d been waiting for an hour when he finally turned up</i> (up to a moment in the past). Two main uses:</p><ul><li><b>Duration up to a past moment</b>, often with <i>for, since, how long, all day</i>: <i>They’d been living together for six years when they got married.</i></li><li><b>A past situation explained by an earlier activity</b> whose effects were visible: <i>Her eyes were red. She’d been crying.</i> <i>The ground was wet: it had been raining.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="ex"><p>Past perfect simple = a completed result, how much or how many: <i>By noon she <b>had written</b> three emails.</i><br>Past perfect continuous = an activity, how long: <i>She <b>had been writing</b> emails all morning.</i><br>State verbs (<i>know, believe, own, be, like</i>) stay simple: <i>We <b>had known</b> each other for years</i>, not <s>had been knowing</s>.</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> « J’attendais depuis une heure quand il est arrivé » ne se traduit <b>pas</b> par <s>I was waiting since an hour</s>. Le français met l’imparfait avec « depuis » ; l’anglais exige le past perfect (continu) avec <i>for</i> (durée) ou <i>since</i> (point de départ) : <i>I <b>had been waiting for</b> an hour when he arrived.</i></p></div>`,
  audio:'I had been waiting for an hour when he finally turned up. Her eyes were red. She had been crying. We had known each other for years.'},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'Translating « depuis » + imparfait',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> a French sentence in the past contains <i>depuis</i> (or <i>ça faisait… que</i>) with an imperfect: « il travaillait là depuis… », « ça faisait deux ans qu’on se connaissait ». <b>Why:</b> English sees an action that started before a past reference point and was still going on at that point, which is the job of the past perfect. <b>How:</b></p>
<ol><li>Find the past reference point (often a clause with <i>when</i> or <i>before</i>, or a date). It stays in the past simple.</li><li>Is the verb an activity (<i>work, live, wait, learn</i>) or a state (<i>know, be, have, own</i>)? Activity → <i>had been + -ing</i>; state → <i>had + past participle</i>.</li><li>Duration → <b>for</b> (<i>for three years</i>); starting point → <b>since</b> (<i>since 2015, since the summer</i>).</li></ol>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Translate: a) « Quand je l’ai rencontrée, elle vivait à Rome depuis trois ans. » b) « Ils se connaissaient depuis l’école primaire. »',
   etapes:[{f:'a) Reference point: <i>When I met her</i> (past simple).',w:'That is the moment we look back from.'},
    {f:'a) <i>vivre</i> is an activity and <i>trois ans</i> is a duration: <i>had been living … for three years</i>.',w:'Activity + duration → past perfect continuous + <i>for</i>.'},
    {f:'a) <i>When I met her, she had been living in Rome for three years.</i>',w:'In speech: <i>she’d been living</i>.'},
    {f:'b) <i>se connaître</i> = <i>know each other</i>, a state verb, and <i>l’école primaire</i> is a starting point: <i>They had known each other since primary school.</i>',w:'State → past perfect simple; starting point → <i>since</i>.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>When I met her, she had been living in Rome for three years. They had known each other since primary school.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: depuis + imparfait',
  p:'Translate into English: « J’attendais depuis vingt minutes quand le bus est enfin arrivé. » <b>Question:</b> how do you say it?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:[...V('I{h} been waiting for {n} minutes when the bus {f}.',{h:[' had','’d'],n:['twenty','20'],f:['finally arrived','finally came','arrived at last','came at last','eventually arrived','finally turned up','finally showed up']}),...V('When the bus {f}, I{h} been waiting for {n} minutes.',{f:['finally arrived','finally came','arrived at last','eventually arrived'],h:[' had','’d'],n:['twenty','20']})],
  why:'<i>I had been waiting for twenty minutes when the bus finally arrived.</i> « depuis + durée » → <i>for</i>, and the imperfect becomes the past perfect continuous. <s>I was waiting since twenty minutes</s> is a classic error.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: visible evidence',
  p:'Complete with the past perfect continuous of <i>paint</i>: <i>When I got home, Dad’s hands were covered in blue. He ___ the garden fence all afternoon.</i> <b>Question:</b> what are the missing words?', lab:'Missing words',
  txt:['had been painting','He had been painting','He’d been painting'],
  why:'<i>He had been painting the fence</i>: the blue hands are the visible result of an activity that had been going on up to the moment I got home.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: simple or continuous?',
  p:'Complete with <i>write</i> in the right form of the past perfect: <i>By the time she retired, the novelist ___ twenty-two books.</i> <b>Question:</b> what are the missing words?', lab:'Missing words',
  txt:['had written','she had written','she’d written'],
  why:'A number of finished books (<i>twenty-two</i>) → past perfect <b>simple</b>: <i>had written</i>. The continuous (<i>had been writing</i>) answers “for how long?”, not “how many?”.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Would and used to: habits and states in the past',
  p:`<p>Ruth describes her old routine: <i>I’d get up at six, I’d be on the Tube by seven</i>, and adds <i>I used to think that was perfectly normal.</i> Both <i>would</i> and <i>used to</i> describe a past that is <b>over</b>, but they are not interchangeable.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>used to + base form</b>: past habits <b>and</b> past states that are no longer true. <i>I used to smoke. We used to live in Lyon. She used to have long hair.</i></li><li><b>would + base form</b>: repeated <b>actions</b> only, never states. <i>Every Sunday, my grandfather would take us fishing.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p><i>would</i> is a storyteller’s form: it is typical of nostalgic, descriptive narratives, and it needs the time frame to be set first (<i>When I was a child…, Every summer…</i>). A natural pattern is to open with <i>used to</i> and continue with <i>would</i>: <i>We used to spend August in Brittany. We’d rent the same cottage, and my father would spend all day on the beach.</i> For a single event, or to say how many times, use the past simple: <i>I went to Brittany three times</i>, not <s>I used to go there three times</s>.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p>Negative: <i>I <b>didn’t use to</b> like olives</i> (more formal British English: <i>I used not to</i>). Question: <i><b>Did you use to</b> play an instrument?</i> No <i>-d</i> after <i>did</i>, because <i>did</i> already carries the past.</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> <i>used to</i> n’a pas de présent. « J’ai l’habitude de me lever tôt » se dit <i>I <b>usually</b> get up early</i>, et non <s>I use to get up early</s>. Et l’imparfait français ne se traduit pas toujours par <i>used to</i> : pour décrire une scène ponctuelle, le past simple ou continu suffit (<i>It was raining</i>, pas <s>It used to rain</s>).</p></div>`,
  audio:'We used to spend August in Brittany. We would rent the same cottage, and my father would spend all day on the beach. I didn’t use to like olives.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Spot the mistake',
  p:'Three of these sentences are correct. <b>Question:</b> which one contains a mistake?',
  opts:['When I was a child, we would go camping every summer.','My grandparents used to own a small farm.','Did you use to walk to school?','As a student, I would have a red bicycle.'], ans:3,
  why:'<i>have</i> (= possess) is a state, so <i>would</i> is impossible: <i>As a student, I used to have a red bicycle</i> (or simply <i>I had</i>). <i>would</i> only works for repeated actions.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: didn’t use to',
  p:'Rewrite the first part of this sentence with <i>didn’t use to</i>: <i>When she was younger, Ana never ate fish, but now she loves it.</i> Write only <i>When she was younger, Ana…</i> up to <i>fish</i>. <b>Question:</b> what do you write?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:V('When she was younger, Ana {n} eat fish.',{n:['didn’t use to','used not to','never used to']}),
  why:'<i>When she was younger, Ana didn’t use to eat fish.</i> After <i>did / didn’t</i>, write <i>use</i>, not <i>used</i>: the past is already in <i>did</i>. (<i>didn’t used to</i> is common in informal writing, but is considered incorrect.)'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Be used to and get used to',
  p:`<p>Ruth says <i>I <b>wasn’t used to</b> the silence</i>, the host replies <i>you <b>got used to</b> it</i>, and Ruth concludes <i>Now <b>I’m</b> so <b>used to</b> it…</i>. Here <i>used</i> is an adjective meaning “accustomed”. It has nothing to do with past habits, and these expressions work in every tense.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>be used to + noun / -ing</b> = être habitué à (a state): <i>I’m used to <b>working</b> nights. She isn’t used to the cold.</i></li><li><b>get used to + noun / -ing</b> = s’habituer à (a process): <i>You’ll soon get used to <b>driving</b> on the left. It took me months to get used to the food.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>The <i>to</i> in these expressions is a <b>preposition</b>, not part of an infinitive. That is why the verb that follows takes <i>-ing</i>, exactly as after <i>look forward to</i> or <i>object to</i>. Test: could you put a noun here? <i>I’m used to <b>the noise</b></i> works, so <i>I’m used to <b>living</b> here</i>.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>I used to live alone.</i> = I lived alone in the past, but not any more.<br><i>I’m used to living alone.</i> = Living alone is normal for me; it doesn’t bother me.<br><i>I’m getting used to living alone.</i> = It is gradually becoming normal for me.</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> <s>I am used to work late</s> est faux : après <i>be / get used to</i>, le verbe prend <i>-ing</i> : <i>I’m used to <b>working</b> late.</i> Et « avoir l’habitude de » se traduit rarement par <i>have the habit of</i> : selon le sens, dis <i>be used to + -ing</i> (c’est familier pour moi) ou <i>usually</i> (je le fais souvent).</p></div>`,
  audio:'I used to live alone. I’m used to living alone. I’m getting used to living alone.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: getting used to it',
  p:'Translate into English: « Je n’arrive pas à m’habituer à conduire à gauche. » <b>Question:</b> how do you say it?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:V('I {c} get used to driving on the left{e}.',{c:['can’t','just can’t','still can’t','can’t seem to','just can’t seem to'],e:['',' side',' hand side',' of the road',' side of the road',' hand side of the road']}),
  why:'<i>I can’t get used to driving on the left.</i> <i>get used to</i> is the process of becoming accustomed, and <i>to</i> is a preposition, so <i>driving</i>, not <s>drive</s>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: which used to?',
  p:'Complete with the right expression: <i>used to</i>, <i>be used to</i> or <i>get used to</i>, in the right form. <i>Nurses on permanent night shifts often say that they never really ___ sleeping during the day.</i> <b>Question:</b> what are the missing words?', lab:'Missing words',
  txt:['get used to','got used to'],
  why:'A process that never quite succeeds: <i>they never really get used to sleeping during the day</i>. After <i>get used to</i>, <i>sleeping</i> takes <i>-ing</i>. <i>used to sleeping</i> alone would be impossible.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'Building a richer story',
  p:`<p>At B2 level, a story is more than a list of events in the past simple. Good storytellers move back and forth in time, explain causes, and build up to a moment of realisation. The tenses you know already do most of the work; a few <b>time markers</b> and structures do the rest.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>By the time</b> + past simple, then past perfect: <i>By the time the ambulance arrived, he had stopped breathing.</i></li><li><b>Until then / up to that point</b>: <i>Until then, I had never been abroad.</i></li><li><b>It was only when… that…</b> for a delayed realisation: <i>It was only when I got home that I realised I’d lost my keys.</i></li><li><b>all along</b> = from the start, without anyone knowing: <i>The answer had been in front of us all along.</i></li><li><b>eventually</b> = in the end, after a long time: <i>Eventually, I found a job.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="ex"><p>Flat: <i>I worked in a bank. I was unhappy. I left. I became a baker.</i><br>Rich: <i>I had been working in a bank for eight years when it finally dawned on me that I was miserable. Until then, I had always assumed that everyone hated their job. It was only when a friend dragged me along to a bread-making course that I realised what I really wanted to do. Eventually, I opened my own bakery.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> <i>eventually</i> ne veut pas dire « éventuellement » (= <i>possibly</i>), mais « finalement, au bout du compte ». Et <i>actually</i> veut dire « en fait », pas « actuellement » (= <i>currently, at the moment</i>).</p></div>`,
  audio:'I had been working in a bank for eight years when it finally dawned on me that I was miserable. Until then, I had always assumed that everyone hated their job. It was only when a friend dragged me along to a bread-making course that I realised what I really wanted to do. Eventually, I opened my own bakery.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Put the realisation in order',
  p:'Tap the words in the right order. <b>Question:</b> how does the storyteller describe the moment she finally understood?',
  ordre:['It was only','when I','read the letter','that I realised','what had','been going on.'],
  why:'<i>It was only when I read the letter that I realised what had been going on.</i> The structure <i>It was only when… that…</i> puts the moment of realisation in focus, and <i>had been going on</i> describes an activity in progress before that moment.'},

 {kind:'Prononciation', h:'Used to: two pronunciations',
  p:`<p>Two words are spelt <i>used</i>, but they are not pronounced the same way.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>used to</b> (past habit) and <b>be / get used to</b> (accustomed): the <i>s</i> is a hard /s/ and <i>to</i> is weak: /ˈjuːstə/, almost one word, “yoosta”. <i>I used to live there. I’m used to it.</i></li><li><b>used</b> (= utilisé, the past of <i>use</i>): the <i>s</i> is a /z/ and the <i>-ed</i> a /d/: /juːzd/. <i>I used a knife. It’s a used car.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> ne prononce pas le <i>-ed</i> comme une syllabe (<s>“you-zed”</s>) et ne sépare pas <i>used</i> et <i>to</i> : dans <i>used to</i>, le <i>d</i> disparaît complètement et le <i>s</i> reste sourd.</p></div>
<p>Listen to the contrast, then repeat the sentence.</p>`,
  audio:'I used to live there. I’m used to it. I used a knife. It’s a used car.',
  dire:'I used to live in London, so I’m used to the noise.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'Reading: the corner shop',
  p:`<p>Read this extract from a personal essay, then answer. You can also listen to it.</p><div class="blk" data-t="The corner shop">${paras(READ_PAST)}</div><p><b>Question:</b> why had the grandparents delayed their retirement?</p>`,
  audio:READ_PAST,
  opts:['They needed the money because business was so bad.','They were waiting for the supermarket to close.','They were worried about who would look after their customers.','Their grandchild wanted to take over the shop.'], ans:2,
  why:'<i>They had kept putting it off because they couldn’t imagine who would look after their customers.</i> Notice the tenses: <i>would spend</i> and <i>used to know</i> (old habits and states), <i>had been getting worse</i> and <i>had been working</i> (activities up to the closure in 2009), <i>It was only years later… that I understood</i> (delayed realisation).'},

 {kind:'Dictée', h:'Dictation: a hidden talent',
  p:'Listen and write exactly what you hear. You can listen as many times as you like and use “Plus lentement”.',
  dictee:'She had been working there for years before anyone noticed how talented she was.',
  why:'<i>had been working</i>: an activity that lasted up to a past moment. Notice the indirect structure at the end: <i>how talented she was</i>, not <s>how talented was she</s>.'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Talk about a change in your life',
  p:'Speak for one to two minutes about a big change in your life: a move, a new school or job, a new routine. Say what your life used to be like, what you had been doing before the change, what took some getting used to, and how you feel about it now.',
  oral:{modele:'Three years ago, I moved from a small town in the Alps to Zurich for my studies. Back then, I used to know everyone in my street, and every Saturday my friends and I would go skiing or walking in the mountains. I had been living in the same house since I was born, so leaving was a real turning point. The city took some getting used to. At first I wasn’t used to the crowds, the noise, or the fact that nobody said hello on the stairs. I had second thoughts more than once. Eventually, though, I settled in. I found a climbing club, made new friends, and now I’m so used to city life that my home town feels incredibly quiet when I go back. Looking back, I’m glad I made the move.',
   criteres:['I used used to or would for old habits or states','I used the past perfect continuous (had been + -ing) at least once','I used be used to or get used to + noun or -ing','I used at least one time marker (back then, eventually, looking back, by the time…)','I spoke for over a minute, telling a story with a beginning, a turning point and an end']}},

 {kind:'Écriture', h:'Write about a turning point',
  p:'A website collects short personal stories under the title <i>The day everything changed</i>. Write your story in about 180 words. Set the scene (what had been happening before), describe the turning point, and explain how your life changed. Use the past perfect continuous at least once, <i>used to</i> or <i>would</i> for past habits, and <i>be used to</i> or <i>get used to</i>.',
  ecrire:{min:160, modele:'<p>For three years after university, I had been working in a call centre, answering complaints about broken washing machines. Every morning I would catch the same bus, sit at the same desk and listen to the same angry customers. I used to tell myself it was only temporary, but I had stopped believing it a long time before.</p><p>Then, one Tuesday in March, a customer I had been talking to for twenty minutes suddenly asked me where I was from. When I told her I was French, she switched to perfect French and explained that she was looking for a translator for her small publishing company. It was only when I put the phone down that I realised she had offered me an interview.</p><p>I got the job. The first months were hard: I wasn’t used to working alone, and it took me a long time to get used to the deadlines. But I have never regretted it. Looking back, that phone call was the turning point of my working life, and it came completely out of the blue.</p>',
   criteres:[{t:'I used the past perfect continuous (had been + -ing)', re:'\\b(had|[’\']d) been \\w+ing\\b'},{t:'I used used to or would for past habits', re:'\\bused to \\w+|\\bwould\\b|[’\']d (get|go|spend|sit|take|walk|play|catch|come|have)\\b'},{t:'I used be used to or get used to', re:'\\b(be|am|is|are|was|were|been|being|get|gets|got|getting|gotten)(n[’\']t)?\\s+(really\\s+|never\\s+|not\\s+|so\\s+)?used to\\b|[’\'](m|s|re)\\s+(not\\s+|so\\s+)?used to\\b'},{t:'I used at least one time marker (by the time, until then, it was only when, eventually, looking back…)', re:'\\b(by the time|until then|it was only when|eventually|looking back|all along|back then)\\b'},'My story has a clear before, turning point and after','I wrote about 180 words']}},

 {kind:'Récapitulatif', h:'What to remember',
  p:`<div class="keep"><ul><li><b>Past perfect continuous</b> (<i>had been + -ing</i>): an activity lasting up to a past moment (<i>I’d been waiting for an hour when he came</i>), or explaining a past situation (<i>Her eyes were red: she’d been crying</i>). For a number or a finished result, the past perfect simple (<i>had written three books</i>); state verbs stay simple (<i>had known</i>).</li><li><b>depuis + imparfait</b> → past perfect (continuous) + <i>for</i> (durée) ou <i>since</i> (point de départ) : <i>She had been living in Rome for three years.</i></li><li><b>used to</b> + base form: past habits and states. <b>would</b>: repeated past actions only, once the time frame is set. Negative <i>didn’t use to</i>, question <i>Did you use to…?</i> No present: « j’ai l’habitude de » → <i>usually</i>.</li><li><b>be used to</b> (state) / <b>get used to</b> (process) + noun or <b>-ing</b>: <i>I’m used to working nights. You’ll get used to it.</i></li><li>Story markers: <i>by the time, until then, it was only when… that…, all along, eventually</i> (= finalement, not éventuellement), <i>looking back, back then</i>.</li><li>Pronunciation: <i>used to</i> /ˈjuːstə/; <i>used</i> (utilisé) /juːzd/.</li></ul></div>`},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'A call to the insurance company',
  p:'Mr Clarke is calling his home insurance company. Listen to the conversation, then answer the questions one by one. You can listen again at any time.',
  audio:[['Agent','Home insurance claims, Joanna speaking. How can I help?'],['Mr Clarke','Hello. I need to report some water damage in my flat. I got back from a business trip on Sunday night and part of the kitchen ceiling had collapsed.'],['Agent','Oh dear, I’m sorry to hear that. Do you know what caused it?'],['Mr Clarke','The plumber says a pipe in the flat upstairs had been leaking for at least a week. The neighbours were away too, so nobody noticed.'],['Agent','I see. And how long had you been away?'],['Mr Clarke','Ten days. I’d been in Singapore for work.'],['Agent','Right. Had you ever made a claim with us before?'],['Mr Clarke','No, never. I’ve been with you for six years and I’d never had a problem until now.'],['Agent','OK. I’ll open a claim, and someone will call you tomorrow to arrange a visit.']], cache:true,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) Why did part of the kitchen ceiling collapse?', opts:['Mr Clarke had left a tap running.','A pipe upstairs had been leaking for days.','There had been a violent storm.','The neighbours had been doing building work.'], ans:1, hint:'Listen to what the plumber said.', why:'<i>A pipe in the flat upstairs had been leaking for at least a week</i>: an activity lasting up to the moment of discovery, hence the past perfect continuous.'},
   {q:'b) How many days had Mr Clarke been away? Write the number.', lab:'Days', txt:['10','ten','ten days','10 days'], hint:'The agent asks <i>how long had you been away?</i>', why:'<i>Ten days. I’d been in Singapore for work.</i>'},
   {q:'c) Complete the plumber’s explanation with the past perfect continuous of <i>leak</i>: <i>A pipe ___ for at least a week.</i>', lab:'Missing words', txt:['had been leaking'], hint:'<i>had been</i> + verb-<i>ing</i>.', why:'<i>had been leaking</i>: the leak was in progress for a week up to the moment Mr Clarke came home.'},
   {q:'d) The agent also wants to know how long the neighbours had been away. Write her question.', lab:'Her question', txt:V('How long had {n} neighbours been away{f}?',{n:['the','your','his'],f:['',' for']}), hint:'<i>be away</i> is a state: past perfect simple, <i>had … been away</i>.', why:'<i>How long had the neighbours been away?</i> <i>be</i> is a state verb, so no continuous form.'}],
  sol:'<p>Mr Clarke came home after <b>ten days</b> in Singapore to find that part of his kitchen ceiling had collapsed, because a pipe in the flat upstairs <i>had been leaking</i> for at least a week while the neighbours were away. The agent’s questions use the past perfect because they look back from the moment of discovery: <i>How long had you been away? Had you ever made a claim?</i> And for the neighbours: <i>How long had the neighbours been away?</i> (state verb, simple form).</p>'},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'A family story',
  p:`<p>You asked your aunt about your grandfather’s first years in Canada. Read her email, then answer.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="Email from your aunt"><p>He arrived in Montreal in January 1962, in the middle of a snowstorm. He had been saving for the ticket for two years, and he had never seen snow like it. He didn’t speak a word of French, which came as a shock, because nobody had told him that most of the city did!</p><p>That first winter, he would walk an hour to the factory every morning because he couldn’t afford the bus. He used to say that the cold was the worst part, but I think loneliness was worse. It took him years to get used to being so far from home. Then he met your grandmother at a dance, and the rest, as they say, is history.</p></div>`,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) Which statement is true?', opts:['Grandad spoke French when he arrived in Montreal.','Grandad took the bus to the factory that first winter.','Grandad had been saving up for his ticket for two years before he left.','Grandad got used to living far from home within a few weeks.'], ans:2, hint:'Look for a past perfect continuous in the first paragraph.', why:'<i>He had been saving for the ticket for two years.</i> He walked because he couldn’t afford the bus, didn’t speak French, and took <i>years</i> to get used to being far from home.'},
   {q:'b) Translate into English: « Quand il a rencontré ma grand-mère, il vivait au Canada depuis quatre ans. »', lab:'Your sentence', long:true, txt:[...V('When he met my {g}, he{h} been living in Canada for {n} years.',{g:['grandmother','grandma','granny','nan'],h:[' had','’d'],n:['four','4']}),...V('He{h} been living in Canada for {n} years when he met my {g}.',{h:[' had','’d'],n:['four','4'],g:['grandmother','grandma','granny','nan']})], hint:'« depuis + imparfait » with a duration: <i>had been + -ing</i> + <i>for</i>.', why:'<i>When he met my grandmother, he had been living in Canada for four years.</i> Activity + duration up to a past moment → past perfect continuous with <i>for</i>.'},
   {q:'c) <i>That first winter, he would walk an hour to the factory.</i> Why is <i>would</i> correct here, but not in <i>He would have a tiny room near the port</i>?', opts:['Because would only works with verbs of movement.','Because walking to work is a repeated action, while having a room is a state.','Because the first sentence is in the future.','Because have can never be used after would.'], ans:1, hint:'Think about the difference between a habit and a state.', why:'<i>would</i> describes repeated past <b>actions</b>. For a past <b>state</b>, use <i>used to</i> or the past simple: <i>He used to have / He had a tiny room near the port.</i>'},
   {q:'d) Rewrite with <i>be used to</i>, keeping the beginning <i>After ten years, he…</i>: <i>After ten years, cold winters were normal for him.</i>', lab:'Your sentence', txt:V('After ten years, he {v} used to {t}.',{v:['was','had got','had gotten','had become','was completely','was quite'],t:['cold winters','the cold winters','the cold']}), hint:'Past state: <i>was used to</i> + noun.', why:'<i>After ten years, he was used to cold winters.</i> <i>be used to</i> = être habitué à, followed here by a noun.'}],
  sol:'<p>Your grandfather <i>had been saving</i> for two years before he left, arrived in a snowstorm in 1962 and didn’t speak French. That first winter he <i>would walk</i> to work every morning (repeated action), and he <i>used to say</i> that the cold was the worst part. It took him years to <i>get used to</i> being far from home. Translation: <i>When he met my grandmother, he had been living in Canada for four years.</i> With <i>be used to</i>: <i>After ten years, he was used to cold winters.</i></p>'},
]},

/* ======================================================================= */
{subj:'en', sem:'b2', id:'en-b2-future-perfect', o:3, h:'Looking ahead', p:'Talk about your career and the future with precision: the future continuous and future perfect, be about to and be due to, and the vocabulary of ambitions and career moves.', steps:[
 {kind:'Écoute', h:'Five years from now',
  p:`<p>By the end of this unit, you will be able to say what you will be doing and what you will have achieved at a given point in the future, talk about scheduled and imminent events, and discuss career plans convincingly.</p>
<p>Leo, a software developer, has asked his manager Claire for a career conversation. <b>Listen without reading</b>, twice if you need to.</p>
<p><b>Question:</b> why has Leo asked to meet Claire?</p>`,
  audio:[['Claire','So, Leo, where do you see yourself in five years’ time?'],['Leo','Honestly? Leading a small team. By then I’ll have been working in software for almost eight years, so it feels realistic.'],['Claire','It does. What’s standing in the way?'],['Leo','Management experience, mainly. That’s partly why I asked to meet. The Lisbon project is about to start, isn’t it?'],['Claire','It is. The kick-off is due to take place on the third of March, and they’ll be looking for someone to coordinate the junior developers.'],['Leo','That’s exactly what I was hoping to hear. Will you be putting names forward?'],['Claire','I will. But think it through: you’ll be spending at least one week a month in Lisbon.'],['Leo','That’s fine. And my certification course will have finished by February, so the timing works.'],['Claire','Then I’ll mention you to the project lead this week. Don’t get your hopes up just yet, though. Nothing’s been decided.']], cache:true,
  opts:['He wants to move to Lisbon permanently.','He hopes to be considered for a coordinating role on the Lisbon project.','He is about to hand in his notice.','He needs time off to finish a certification course.'], ans:1,
  why:'Leo lacks management experience, and the Lisbon project needs <i>someone to coordinate the junior developers</i>. He asks <i>Will you be putting names forward?</i> His course <i>will have finished by February</i>, so it isn’t a problem.'},

 {kind:'Vocabulaire', h:'Careers and ambitions',
  p:`<p>The language of career plans and moves. Tap a line to hear it. Many of these are fixed collocations: we <i>climb</i> the career ladder, <i>hand in</i> our notice, <i>meet</i> a deadline. They will come back in your vocabulary review.</p>`,
  mots:[['to climb the career ladder','gravir les échelons','She climbed the career ladder faster than anyone.'],['to take on more responsibility','assumer davantage de responsabilités','I’m ready to take on more responsibility.'],['to put someone’s name forward','proposer quelqu’un (pour un poste)','My manager put my name forward for the project.'],['to get your hopes up','se faire des illusions, trop espérer','Don’t get your hopes up: nothing’s been decided yet.'],['to be on track (to)','être en bonne voie (pour)','We’re on track to finish by Friday.'],['to meet a deadline','respecter un délai','We worked all weekend to meet the deadline.'],['to think something through','bien réfléchir à quelque chose','Think it through before you resign.'],['to keep your options open','ne fermer aucune porte','I’m keeping my options open for now.'],['a stepping stone','un tremplin','This job is a stepping stone to something bigger.'],['in the long run','à long terme','In the long run, the training will pay off.'],['in five years’ time','dans cinq ans','In five years’ time, I’d like to be running my own business.'],['to hand in your notice','donner sa démission','He handed in his notice on Monday.'],['to retrain as','se reconvertir comme','She retrained as a nurse at forty.'],['to go freelance','se mettre à son compte','After ten years in an agency, he went freelance.'],['job security','la sécurité de l’emploi','Freelancing offers freedom but little job security.'],['prospects','perspectives d’avenir','The job is badly paid, but the prospects are excellent.'],['a career break','une pause dans sa carrière','She took a two-year career break to travel.']],
  note:'<b>Faux amis :</b> « une formation » se dit <i>training</i> ou <i>a course</i>, pas <i>a formation</i>. « Postuler » se dit <i>apply for</i> (<i>I applied for the job</i>), pas <i>postulate</i>. Et <i>career</i> (/kəˈrɪə/, accent sur la 2e syllabe) ne se confond pas avec <i>carrier</i> (transporteur).'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: don’t get your hopes up',
  p:'Translate into English, using an expression from the list: « Ne te fais pas trop d’illusions, rien n’est encore décidé. » <b>Question:</b> how do you say it?', lab:'Your sentence', long:true,
  txt:V('Don’t get your hopes up{y}, nothing{h} been decided yet.',{y:['',' too much',' just yet',' yet'],h:[' has','’s']}),
  why:'<i>Don’t get your hopes up, nothing has been decided yet.</i> The present perfect passive <i>has been decided</i> + <i>yet</i> = « n’est pas encore décidé ».'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'The future continuous: in progress at a future moment',
  p:`<p>Claire warns Leo: <i>you’ll be spending at least one week a month in Lisbon</i>, and says the project <i>will be looking for someone</i>. Neither sentence describes a single future event: each one describes an activity <b>in progress</b> around a point in the future. That is the job of the <b>future continuous</b>.</p>
<div class="def"><p><b>Form:</b> <i>will be</i> + verb-<i>ing</i> (<i>I’ll be working, she won’t be coming, Will you be using…?</i>).</p><ul><li><b>In progress at a future moment</b>: <i>This time next week, I’ll be lying on a beach.</i> <i>Don’t call at eight: we’ll be having dinner.</i></li><li><b>Something that will happen in the normal course of events</b>, not because of a new decision: <i>I’ll be seeing Anna tomorrow anyway, so I can give her the keys.</i></li><li><b>Polite questions about someone’s plans</b>, less pushy than a direct request: <i>Will you be using the car tonight?</i> (= I’d like to borrow it, but I’m not asking directly.)</li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>Picture a timeline with a point on it (<i>at eight tomorrow, this time next year</i>). The future simple puts an event <b>on</b> the point; the future continuous stretches an activity <b>across</b> it: you are already in the middle of it when the point arrives.</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>When you land, I’ll wait at the exit</i> (a decision, a promise).<br><i>When you land, I’ll be waiting at the exit</i> (I’ll already be there, in the middle of waiting).</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> le français n’a pas de futur progressif. « Demain à cette heure-ci, je serai en train de passer mon examen » se dit simplement <i>This time tomorrow, I’ll be taking my exam</i> (et « passer un examen » = <i>take</i>, pas <s>pass</s>, qui veut dire « réussir »). Comme les autres formes progressives, le futur continu ne s’emploie pas avec les verbes d’état : <s>I’ll be knowing</s>.</p></div>`,
  audio:'This time next week, I’ll be lying on a beach. Don’t call at eight: we’ll be having dinner. Will you be using the car tonight?'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: this time tomorrow',
  p:'Complete with the future continuous of <i>fly</i>: <i>Don’t try to call me at ten tomorrow morning: I ___ to Lisbon.</i> <b>Question:</b> what are the missing words?', lab:'Missing words',
  txt:['will be flying','I will be flying'],
  why:'<i>I’ll be flying to Lisbon</i>: at ten, the flight will be in progress. <i>I will fly</i> would only announce the event.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: a polite question about plans',
  p:'You would like to borrow your flatmate’s bike on Saturday, but you don’t want to ask directly. Ask her politely, with the future continuous, whether she’ll be using it on Saturday. <b>Question:</b> what do you say?', lab:'Your question',
  txt:[...V('Will you be using {o} on Saturday?',{o:['your bike','the bike','it','your bike yourself']}),...V('Will you be needing {o} on Saturday?',{o:['your bike','the bike','it']})],
  why:'<i>Will you be using your bike on Saturday?</i> It asks about her plans, not for a favour, so she can say no without feeling she has refused you anything. Very English.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'The future perfect: done before a deadline',
  p:`<p>Leo says: <i>my certification course <b>will have finished</b> by February</i> and <i>By then I’<b>ll have been working</b> in software for almost eight years.</i> He projects himself to a moment in the future and looks <b>back</b> from it. That is what the future perfect does.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>Future perfect simple</b>: <i>will have</i> + past participle. An action <b>completed before</b> a future moment: <i>By the time you read this, I’ll have left.</i> <i>We won’t have finished by Friday.</i></li><li><b>Future perfect continuous</b>: <i>will have been</i> + -<i>ing</i>. The <b>duration</b> of an activity up to a future moment: <i>In June, I’ll have been working here for ten years.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>These tenses nearly always come with a time expression that sets the future reference point: <b>by</b> + time (<i>by Monday, by then, by the end of the year</i>), <b>by the time</b> + clause, <b>in … time</b>. <i>by</i> means “at the latest”, not “until”: <i>I’ll have finished <b>by</b> six</i> (at some point before six) vs <i>I’ll be working <b>until</b> six</i> (continuously up to six).</p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>By 2030, the company will have opened twenty new stores.</i> (how many: completed)<br><i>By 2030, the company will have been operating for a century.</i> (how long: an activity)</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> après <i>when, by the time, as soon as, before, after, until</i>, l’anglais n’emploie jamais <i>will</i>, même si le français met un futur : « Quand tu arriveras, j’aurai fini » → <i>When you <b>arrive</b>, I’ll have finished</i>, et non <s>when you will arrive</s>. Le futur antérieur français devient alors un present perfect : « dès que j’aurai fini » → <i>as soon as I <b>have finished</b></i>. Et « d’ici vendredi » se dit <i>by Friday</i>, pas <s>until Friday</s>.</p></div>`,
  audio:'By the time you read this, I’ll have left. In June, I’ll have been working here for ten years. When you arrive, I’ll have finished.'},

 {kind:'Méthode', h:'Choosing a future form from a timeline',
  p:`<p><b>When:</b> you need to say where you will be, or what you will have done, at a precise moment in the future: a deadline, an interview, an anniversary. <b>Why it works:</b> each future form has a fixed relationship with the reference point; once you have found the point, the choice is almost mechanical. <b>How:</b></p>
<ol><li>Find the reference point: <i>at 10 tomorrow, by Friday, this time next year, by the time…</i></li><li>Is the action <b>in progress</b> at that point? → future continuous (<i>will be doing</i>).</li><li>Is it <b>finished before</b> that point? → future perfect (<i>will have done</i>).</li><li>Are you measuring <b>how long</b> it will have lasted at that point? → future perfect continuous (<i>will have been doing</i>).</li><li>In the time clause itself (<i>when…, by the time…</i>), use a present tense.</li></ol>`,
  demo:{enonce:'Maya’s Thursday: she gives her talk from 10.00 to 10.45; she has been preparing it since Monday; her train leaves at 17.00. Complete: a) At 10.30, Maya ___ (speak). b) By 11.00, she ___ (give) her talk. c) By Thursday morning, she ___ (prepare) it for three days. d) By the time her train ___ (leave), she ___ (see) a dozen other talks.',
   etapes:[{f:'a) 10.30 falls in the middle of 10.00–10.45: <i>At 10.30, Maya will be speaking.</i>',w:'In progress at the reference point → future continuous.'},
    {f:'b) 11.00 comes after 10.45: <i>By 11.00, she will have given her talk.</i>',w:'Completed before the point → future perfect simple. <i>given</i> is the past participle of <i>give</i>.'},
    {f:'c) From Monday to Thursday, measured as a duration: <i>she will have been preparing it for three days.</i>',w:'How long, up to the point → future perfect continuous, with <i>for</i>.'},
    {f:'d) <i>By the time her train <b>leaves</b>, she will have seen a dozen other talks.</i>',w:'Present simple in the <i>by the time</i> clause, future perfect in the main clause.'}],
   conclusion:'<i>At 10.30, Maya will be speaking. By 11.00, she will have given her talk. By Thursday morning, she will have been preparing it for three days. By the time her train leaves, she will have seen a dozen other talks.</i>'}},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: by the end of the month',
  p:'Complete with the future perfect of <i>move</i>: <i>Don’t send anything to our old address: by the end of the month, we ___ into the new office.</i> <b>Question:</b> what are the missing words?', lab:'Missing words',
  txt:['will have moved','we will have moved'],
  why:'<i>we’ll have moved</i>: the move will be completed before the end of the month. <i>by</i> = at the latest.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: ten years in June',
  p:'Translate into English with the future perfect continuous: « En juin, ça fera dix ans que je travaille ici. » <b>Question:</b> how do you say it?', lab:'Your sentence',
  txt:[...V('{b} June, I will have been working here for {n} years.',{b:['By','In'],n:['ten','10']}),...V('I will have been working here for {n} years {b} June.',{n:['ten','10'],b:['by','in']})],
  why:'<i>By June, I’ll have been working here for ten years.</i> « ça fera … que » + a duration up to a future moment → <i>will have been + -ing</i> + <i>for</i>.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: after when',
  p:'Only one of these sentences is correct. <b>Question:</b> which one?',
  opts:['I’ll call you when I will have finished.','I’ll call you when I will finish.','I’ll call you when I’ve finished.','I’ll call you until I’ve finished.'], ans:2,
  why:'No <i>will</i> after <i>when</i>: « quand j’aurai fini » → <i>when I’ve finished</i> (present perfect) or <i>when I finish</i>. <i>until</i> means « jusqu’à ce que », which makes no sense here.'},

 {kind:'Grammaire', h:'About to, due to: the near and the scheduled future',
  p:`<p>Leo asks: <i>The Lisbon project <b>is about to</b> start, isn’t it?</i> and Claire answers: <i>The kick-off <b>is due to</b> take place on the third of March.</i> Both refer to the future, with very different nuances.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><b>be about to + base form</b>: something will happen <b>very soon</b>, any minute now. <i>Hurry up, the film’s about to start.</i> Stronger and more formal: <i>be on the point of + -ing</i>: <i>She was on the point of giving up when she got the call.</i></li><li><b>be due to + base form</b>: something is <b>scheduled</b> or expected at a particular time (timetables, deadlines, official events): <i>The train is due to arrive at 10.42.</i> <i>The report is due to be published next week.</i> Without a verb: <i>The baby is due in May. The rent is due on the first.</i></li><li><b>be set to + base form</b>, very common in the news: something looks certain to happen. <i>Prices are set to rise again.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="idea"><p>All three also work in the past, for a future seen from the past, often one that did <b>not</b> happen: <i>I was about to call you</i> (but you called first). <i>The plane was due to land at six, but it was delayed.</i></p></div>
<div class="ex"><p><i>Quick, take a photo! The sun’s about to set.</i><br><i>The new bridge is due to open in 2030, but nobody believes it will.</i></p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> <i>due to</i> a deux sens. <i>The train is due to arrive at six</i> = il doit arriver à six heures (c’est prévu). <i>The train was cancelled due to the strike</i> = à cause de la grève. Et pour « je vais partir » (tout de suite), ne dis pas <i>I will leave</i> : dis <i>I’m about to leave</i> ou <i>I’m just leaving</i>.</p></div>`,
  audio:'Hurry up, the film’s about to start. The train is due to arrive at ten forty-two. Prices are set to rise again.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Your turn: scheduled or imminent?',
  p:'Complete with <i>be due to</i> or <i>be about to</i>, in the right form: <i>According to the timetable, the next ferry ___ leave at 14.15.</i> <b>Question:</b> what are the missing words?', lab:'Missing words',
  txt:['is due to'],
  why:'A timetable = something scheduled → <i>is due to leave</i>. <i>is about to leave</i> would mean “any second now”, which the timetable can’t tell you.'},

 {kind:'Pratique', h:'Put the sentence in order',
  p:'Tap the words in the right order. <b>Question:</b> what do you say when someone phones you just as you were going to phone them?',
  ordre:['I was','just','about to','call you','when','you rang.'],
  why:'<i>I was just about to call you when you rang.</i> <i>was about to</i> describes a future seen from the past that was interrupted. <i>rang</i> (British) = <i>called</i> (American).'},

 {kind:'Prononciation', h:'Weak forms: I’ll have finished',
  p:`<p>In natural speech, the future perfect hardly sounds like three words. <i>will</i> contracts to <i>’ll</i>, and <i>have</i> loses its <i>h</i> and its vowel: /əv/, exactly like the weak form of <i>of</i>. <i>I’ll have finished</i> sounds like “I’ll-uv finished”; <i>she’ll have been working</i> sounds like “she’ll-uv-bin working”, with <i>been</i> reduced to /bɪn/.</p>
<div class="def"><ul><li><i>I’ll have</i> /aɪl əv/ · <i>won’t have</i> /wəʊnt əv/ · <i>been</i> /bɪn/</li><li>The stress falls on the main verb and the time expression: <i>By <b>Fri</b>day, I’ll have <b>fin</b>ished.</i></li></ul></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Piège des francophones :</b> n’accentue pas <i>have</i> : <s>I’ll HAVE finished</s> sonne insistant (« mais si, j’aurai fini ! »). Et à l’écrit, c’est toujours <i>I’ll have</i>, jamais <s>I’ll of</s>, une faute courante même chez les natifs, justement à cause de cette prononciation.</p></div>
<p>Listen, then repeat the sentence with the weak forms.</p>`,
  audio:'By Friday, I’ll have finished. She’ll have been working here for ten years. We won’t have left by then.',
  dire:'By the time you get here, I’ll have finished.'},

 {kind:'Lecture', h:'Reading: the five-year question',
  p:`<p>Read the article, then answer. You can also listen to it.</p><div class="blk" data-t="Article">${paras(READ_FUT)}</div><p><b>Question:</b> according to the article, what makes a convincing answer to the five-year question?</p>`,
  audio:READ_FUT,
  opts:['Naming the exact job title you expect to have.','Saying that you would like the interviewer’s job.','Describing the experience and skills you hope to have gained.','Admitting that you will probably leave soon.'], ans:2,
  why:'<i>The most convincing answers tend to describe skills rather than titles</i>, because they show <i>what you will have learnt, not just where you will be sitting</i>. Note the future perfect and future continuous in the last paragraph: <i>What will you be doing…? What will you have achieved…?</i>'},

 {kind:'Dictée', h:'Dictation: a message before leaving',
  p:'Listen and write exactly what you hear. You can listen as many times as you like and use “Plus lentement”.',
  dictee:'By the time you get this message, I’ll have left for the airport.',
  why:'Present simple after <i>by the time</i> (<i>you get</i>), future perfect in the main clause (<i>I’ll have left</i>). Write <i>I’ll have</i>, never <s>I’ll of</s>.'},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Repeat from memory',
  p:'The sentence is not written down. Listen, then say it from memory, with the weak forms of <i>have</i> and <i>been</i>.',
  dire:{t:'By then I’ll have been working in software for almost eight years.', cache:true}},

 {kind:'Oral', h:'Where do you see yourself?',
  p:'Answer the classic interview question for one to two minutes: <i>Where do you see yourself in five years’ time?</i> Say what you will be doing on an ordinary day, what you will have achieved by then, how long you will have been doing your current activity, and mention one event that is due to happen soon.',
  oral:{modele:'In five years’ time, I hope I’ll be working as a project manager, ideally in an international company. On an ordinary day, I’ll be coordinating a small team and talking to clients, which is something I really enjoy. By then, I’ll have been working in logistics for about nine years, so I’ll have the experience I need. I also hope I’ll have finished a part-time master’s degree: the course is due to start next September, and it lasts two years. I’m about to ask my manager whether the company could support me. Of course, I’m keeping my options open. Things change quickly, and in the long run, what matters most to me is to keep learning.',
   criteres:['I used the future continuous (will be + -ing)','I used the future perfect (will have + past participle)','I used the future perfect continuous with for (will have been + -ing)','I used be about to or be due to','I used career vocabulary (take on responsibility, keep my options open, in the long run…)']}},

 {kind:'Écriture', h:'Write a personal statement',
  p:'Your company has launched a mentoring programme, and you want to apply. Write a personal statement of about 180 words: where you are now, where you see yourself in five years, what you will have achieved by then, and how a mentor would help. Use the future perfect, the future continuous, and <i>be about to</i> or <i>be due to</i>.',
  ecrire:{min:160, modele:'<p>I am applying for the mentoring programme because I am about to reach an important stage in my career. I joined the finance team as a junior analyst, and by the end of this year I will have been working there for three years. In that time I have learnt a great deal, but almost entirely on my own.</p><p>My long-term goal is to lead a small team. In five years’ time, I hope I will be managing four or five analysts and taking on more responsibility for planning. Before that, there are skills I need to develop. By the time I apply for my first management role, I would like to have run at least one project from start to finish, and I will have completed the professional certificate that I am due to start in September.</p><p>A mentor would help me think these plans through and avoid the mistakes that slow many people down. I am realistic: I know that promotions depend on many things. But with the right guidance, I am confident that I will have made real progress by this time next year.</p>',
   criteres:[{t:'I used the future perfect (will have + past participle)', re:'(\\bwill|[’\']ll|\\bwon[’\']t) have (?!been\\b)\\w+'},{t:'I used the future continuous (will be + -ing)', re:'(\\bwill|[’\']ll|\\bwon[’\']t) be \\w+ing\\b'},{t:'I set a future reference point (by the time, by then, by the end of, in … years’ time)', re:'\\b(by the time|by then|by the end of|in \\w+ years[’\']? time|this time next)\\b'},{t:'I used be about to, be due to or be set to', re:'\\b(about to|due to|on the point of|set to)\\b'},'No will after when, by the time or as soon as','I wrote about 180 words in a formal but personal tone']}},

 {kind:'Récapitulatif', h:'What to remember',
  p:`<div class="keep"><ul><li><b>Future continuous</b> (<i>will be + -ing</i>): in progress at a future moment (<i>This time tomorrow I’ll be flying</i>), something that will happen anyway, polite questions about plans (<i>Will you be using the car?</i>).</li><li><b>Future perfect</b> (<i>will have + past participle</i>): completed before a future moment (<i>By Friday, I’ll have finished</i>). <b>Future perfect continuous</b> (<i>will have been + -ing</i>): duration up to a future moment (<i>In June, I’ll have been working here for ten years</i>).</li><li>Method: find the reference point, then ask: in progress? finished before? how long?</li><li><i>by</i> = at the latest; <i>until</i> = continuously up to. No <i>will</i> after <i>when, by the time, as soon as, before, until</i> : « quand j’aurai fini » → <i>when I’ve finished</i>.</li><li><b>be about to</b> (imminent), <b>be on the point of + -ing</b>, <b>be due to</b> (scheduled), <b>be set to</b> (news). In the past: <i>I was about to call you.</i> Attention : <i>due to</i> = aussi « à cause de ».</li><li>Careers: <i>climb the career ladder, take on more responsibility, put someone’s name forward, hand in your notice, retrain as, go freelance, keep your options open, a stepping stone, in the long run</i>.</li><li>Pronunciation: <i>I’ll have</i> /aɪl əv/, <i>been</i> /bɪn/; write <i>have</i>, never <i>of</i>.</li></ul></div>`},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'A voicemail from HR',
  p:'You have just accepted a new job. Listen to the voicemail from the HR department, then answer the questions one by one. You can listen again at any time.',
  audio:'Hello, this is Martin Hale from HR at Kestrel Logistics. I’m calling about your start date. As you know, your contract is due to start on Monday the fourth, but the induction day has been moved to Tuesday, so please come in on Tuesday at nine instead. By the time you arrive, we’ll have set up your laptop and your email account, so you won’t need to bring anything except your passport for the paperwork. For your first two weeks, you’ll be shadowing Kerry Ng in the planning team, and she’ll be showing you how our systems work. Oh, and just a heads-up: we’re about to move to a new building, so the office may be a bit chaotic. If you have any questions, call me back on oh one six one, four nine six, oh seven three two. Thanks, and see you on Tuesday.', cache:true,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) When should you go in for your first day?', opts:['On Monday the 4th at nine','On Tuesday at half past nine','On Monday the 4th at half past nine','On Tuesday at nine'], ans:3, hint:'The contract starts on Monday, but something has been moved.', why:'<i>The induction day has been moved to Tuesday, so please come in on Tuesday at nine instead.</i>'},
   {q:'b) What is the only thing you need to bring? Write one word.', lab:'One word', txt:['passport','your passport','my passport','a passport'], hint:'It’s for the paperwork.', why:'<i>You won’t need to bring anything except your passport for the paperwork.</i>'},
   {q:'c) Complete Martin’s sentence: <i>By the time you arrive, we ___ your laptop.</i>', lab:'Missing words', txt:['will have set up'], hint:'Future perfect of the phrasal verb <i>set up</i>.', why:'<i>we’ll have set up your laptop</i>: it will be done before you arrive. <i>set</i> is irregular: <i>set, set, set</i>.'},
   {q:'d) You call Martin back. Ask him, with the future continuous, whether you will be working from home on Fridays.', lab:'Your question', txt:V('Will I be working from home on {f}?',{f:['Fridays','a Friday','Friday','Fridays at all']}), hint:'<i>Will I be</i> + verb-<i>ing</i>…?', why:'<i>Will I be working from home on Fridays?</i> The future continuous asks about the normal arrangement, politely.'}],
  sol:'<p>Your contract <i>is due to start</i> on Monday the 4th, but you go in on <b>Tuesday at nine</b>. By the time you arrive, they <i>will have set up</i> your laptop and email, so you only bring your passport. For two weeks you <i>will be shadowing</i> Kerry Ng, and the company <i>is about to</i> move to a new building. A polite question about your arrangements: <i>Will I be working from home on Fridays?</i></p>'},

 {kind:'Mission', h:'Planning a career move',
  p:`<p>Your friend Jess has sent you this message. Read it, then answer.</p>
<div class="blk" data-t="Message from Jess"><p>Big news: I’ve decided to retrain as a primary school teacher! I’m about to hand in my notice at the bank. The training course is due to start on 1 September 2027 and lasts eighteen months, including two school placements. I’ve worked out that I can live on my savings until the end of the course, as long as I do some freelance accounting on the side. Scary, but I’ve thought it through. By the time I qualify, I’ll have been working in finance for twelve years, so it really is now or never!</p></div>`,
  parts:[
   {q:'a) The course starts on 1 September 2027 and lasts eighteen months. By the end of which month will Jess have finished it?', opts:['August 2028','December 2028','February 2029','March 2029'], ans:2, hint:'Twelve months take you to the end of August 2028. Add six more.', why:'September 2027 → end of August 2028 = 12 months; + 6 months = end of <b>February 2029</b>. <i>By the end of February 2029, she will have finished the course.</i>'},
   {q:'b) What does Jess mean by <i>I’m about to hand in my notice</i>?', opts:['She resigned a long time ago.','She will resign very soon.','She might resign one day.','The bank has asked her to leave.'], ans:1, hint:'<i>be about to</i> = imminent.', why:'<i>be about to</i> = very soon. <i>hand in your notice</i> = donner sa démission.'},
   {q:'c) Write one sentence with the future perfect: <i>by the end of the course / Jess / complete / two school placements</i>.', lab:'Your sentence', long:true, txt:[...V('By the end of the course, {s} will have {v} two school placements.',{s:['Jess','she'],v:['completed','done','finished']}),...V('{s} will have {v} two school placements by the end of the course.',{s:['Jess','She'],v:['completed','done','finished']})], hint:'<i>will have</i> + past participle.', why:'<i>By the end of the course, Jess will have completed two school placements.</i> Completed before a future point → future perfect simple.'},
   {q:'d) You reply. Ask her, with the future continuous, whether she will be doing freelance accounting during the placements.', lab:'Your question', txt:V('Will you be doing {x} during the {p}?',{x:['freelance accounting','some freelance accounting','any freelance accounting','freelance work','some freelance work','any freelance work'],p:['placements','school placements','placement']}), hint:'<i>Will you be</i> + verb-<i>ing</i>…?', why:'<i>Will you be doing any freelance accounting during the placements?</i> The future continuous asks about her plans neutrally, without sounding worried or critical.'}],
  sol:'<p>Jess <i>is about to</i> resign (very soon). Her course <i>is due to</i> start on 1 September 2027 and lasts 18 months, so she <i>will have finished</i> it by the end of <b>February 2029</b>, after twelve years in finance (<i>she’ll have been working in finance for twelve years</i>). With the future perfect: <i>By the end of the course, Jess will have completed two school placements.</i> A friendly question: <i>Will you be doing any freelance accounting during the placements?</i></p>'},
]},
);
})();
